Description:
P. 128. A skeleton with the head of Hastings, horns sprouting from his forehead, rides a galloping white horse; shafts of flame inscribed 'Desolation' and 'Pestilence' issuing from its nostrils. A coronet falls to the ground; he holds a paper inscribed 'Napaul War'. Flames stream from the left towards and past him, and issue from below a flat tomb-stone under his horse's feet, which is inscribed 'Hic Jacit [sic] The Mortal part of Infamy Avarice and Tyrany'. Two officers and a civilian follow the horse on foot, the civilian clasping the animal's tail and holding up a firebrand. One of the officers (Lionel Smith) wears a belt inscribed 'Vice' (as in No. 12733), and holds up a scourge and a firebrand inscribed 'Discord'; he is clasped by a screaming Fury with snaky hair, and a raised dagger inscribed 'Slander'. The other officer holds a mask. They are hastening towards a building (right), apparently the Residency, Calcutta, outside which is a half length figure of Hastings, as an idol, with a book before him, on a pedestal inscribed 'Search the Scripstures [sic] for therein &. &c.' He reads: "and I looked and beheld a pale horse and his name that sat on him was Death and hell followed with him." On the horizon behind the horse are gibbets and small buildings surrounded by flames.
1 November 1815.
Hand-coloured etching and aquatint;

P. 46. The car of the idol is being drawn and pushed by British officers and soldiers who trample under foot papers inscribed 'Honor' (left) and 'Virtue' (right). Crushed under the front wheels (left), inscribed 'Persecution', 'Injustice', are five Indians, by whom is a paper inscribed 'Napaul'. A skull and bones lie beside them. Under the back wheels (right), inscribed 'Oppression', 'Tyranny', are two British officers (one also trampled on by a fellow-officer) who lie on papers inscribed 'Merit' and 'Humanity'. The body of the car is an oblong chest, open to show Lord Hastings, as the god, seated cross-legged and wearing a loin-cloth with the ribbon and star of the Garter. He has three pairs of arms; one held above his head, a dagger in the left hand, one holding out behind himself a 'Veil of Duplicity', the third with a decanter of wine in the right hand, a mask in the left. On the chest is a platform supporting a round temple, surrounded by niches in each of which stands a tiny nude figure; it is surmounted by a coronet and escutcheon. In front of the temple is a line of arches in each of which is an allegorical figure, larger than those in the temple, but too small for characterization. At each end is a pillar surmounted by a posturing quasi-classical figure, holding spear and sword, and, projecting at each end, a coat of arms with supporters, one a lion, the other a bear, each holding a flag with a St. George's Cross, and apparently burlesquing the Hastings Arms, cf. No. 12731. The two props of this platform flanking Hastings are (left) 'Parliamentary Influence' and (right) 'Ministerial Support'. The car above Hastings is inscribed 'Infamy | MB CCC XV'; below: 'Public Execration | Corruption'. At each end of the car, in the place of the boot or basket of an English coach, is a little rectangular pen with a railing round it. The one in front contains a bishop and a senior officer, and is inscribed 'Episcopacy'; the one behind is inscribed 'Incapacity' and contains one officer and two civilians, apparently members of the Governor-General's Council.
1 October 1815.
Etching and aquatint;
